.Dd $Mdocdate: November 5 2026 $
.Dt SIGNET-HOOKS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm signet-hooks
.Nd external claims hook protocol for signet
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A claims hook is a program that computes application-specific claims, such as
roles or permissions, for a user who has just proved who they are.
.Xr signet 1
stores the answer in the session and returns it from
.Li GET /session
and
.Li POST /api/v1/validate ,
as described in
.Xr signet-api 7 .
.Pp
The hook is configured with the
.Li claims_hook
keys in
.Xr signet-config.yaml 5 .
It is enabled when
.Li claims_hook.enabled
is true and
.Li claims_hook.program
is not empty.
.Ss When the hook runs
Signet starts a new process for each request and never reuses one:
.Bl -bullet
.It
after a correct password, email PIN or security key, before the session is
created;
.It
for
.Li GET /session?claims=refresh ,
with the token of the existing session.
.El
.Ss The process
The program is executed directly, not through a shell, with no arguments.
It runs as the user running signet and inherits signet's environment and working
directory.
A name without a slash is searched for in
.Ev PATH ,
so give an absolute path.
.Pp
The login waits for the hook, so a slow hook is a slow login.
.Ss Request
Signet writes one line of JSON to the hook's standard input and closes it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"type":"claim_request","email":"user@example.com"}
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width email
.It Li type
Always
.Dq claim_request .
.It Li email
The address the user signed in with.
.El
.Pp
A hook should ignore members it does not know,
since a later version may add some.
Versions before 0.13.24 also sent
.Li session_token ,
the session's bearer secret; it is no longer sent.
.Ss Response
The hook writes lines of JSON to its standard output.
Signet reads lines until one has a
.Li type
of
.Dq claim_response
or
.Dq claim_error ,
and ignores JSON lines of any other type.
It then kills the hook and any process it started,
so do any cleanup before writing
the answer.
.Pp
To grant claims:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"type":"claim_response","claims":{"role":"admin","level":3}}
.Ed
.Pp
The value of
.Li claims
is any JSON object.
Without it, the claims are empty.
.Pp
To report a failure, which also refuses the login under
.Dq fail-closed :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"type":"claim_error","error":"no such department"}
.Ed
.Ss Failures
Each of these is a failure, and is logged at warn level:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the program cannot be started;
.It
it answers
.Dq claim_error ;
.It
it writes a line that is not JSON, even before a valid answer;
.It
it writes a line longer than 64 KiB;
.It
it exits without answering, which fails at once without waiting for the timeout;
.It
it has not answered after
.Li claims_hook.timeout_seconds ,
or the client disconnects, and the process is then killed.
.El
.Pp
.Li claims_hook.on_failure
decides what a failure means:
.Bl -tag -width "fail-closed"
.It Dq fail-closed
The default.
During a login no session is created, and the client gets
.Li 403
with the error
.Dq Sign-in refused ,
whichever method was used.
A hook can use this to deny a user.
During a refresh the stored claims are kept and the reply has
.Li claims_refreshed
set to false.
.It Dq fail-open
During a login the session is created with empty claims.
During a refresh the stored claims are replaced with empty ones,
and the reply has
.Li claims_refreshed
set to true.
.El
.Ss Diagnostics
Signet discards the hook's standard error.
To keep debug output, have the hook write it somewhere itself, such as
.Xr syslog 3
or its own file.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/share/examples/signet/hooks/
Example hooks on
.Ox ,
with
.Pa test-hooks.sh ,
which checks that they answer the protocol.
.It Pa /usr/share/doc/signet/examples/hooks/
The same on Linux.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A configuration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
claims_hook:
  enabled: true
  program: "/usr/local/libexec/signet-claims"
  timeout_seconds: 5
  on_failure: "fail-closed"
.Ed
.Pp
A minimal hook that gives everyone the same claim.
It reads the request, answers and exits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/bin/sh
read -r request
echo '{"type":"claim_response","claims":{"role":"user"}}'
.Ed
.Pp
Trying a hook by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo '{"type":"claim_request","email":"a@example.com"}' |
    /usr/local/libexec/signet-claims
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr signet 1 ,
.Xr signet-config.yaml 5 ,
.Xr signet-template 5 ,
.Xr signet-api 7
.Sh CAVEATS
Signet runs the hook in its own process group and kills the whole group,
so anything
the hook started ends with it.
Only a process that moves to a new session or process group survives.
.Pp
Claims are stored in the session when it is created,
and change only when a client
asks for
.Li claims=refresh ,
at most
.Va claims_hook.refresh_rate_limit
times per account in
.Va claims_hook.refresh_rate_window
seconds.
